add(FIGURE("figures/integrated/Figure4_TME_CIBERSORTx_Job14.png",
  "**Figure 4. Tumour immune microenvironment of HPV+ versus HPV− TCGA-HNSC tumours.** CIBERSORTx (LM22) estimated fractions of (a) plasma cells, (b) CD8+ T cells, (c) M0 macrophages, (d) resting NK cells and (e) resting CD4+ memory T cells, the five populations that differed after Benjamini–Hochberg correction across 22 cell types (FDR shown above each panel). (f) Exploratory log~2~ CD8/M2 macrophage ratio. Boxes show the median and interquartile range; points are individual tumours (36 HPV+, 243 HPV−).", 640));

add(H2("3.11. The meiotic programme is confined to malignant cells"));
add(P("All the cohorts above comprise bulk tissue, and HPV+ tumours are richer in lymphocytes and plasma cells (Section 3.10). The meiotic signal could therefore reflect a larger population of non-malignant cells that constitutively express meiotic genes rather than reactivation within tumour cells. This was tested in GSE181919, a single-cell HNSCC atlas annotated for HPV status and malignant versus stromal identity. After restriction to primary tumours, 2,463 malignant cells from 7 HPV+ patients, 2,366 malignant cells from 13 HPV− patients and 18,259 non-malignant cells from the same tumours were available. Published cell-type annotations were re-derived from canonical markers, confirming all nine reported cell types. Malignant cells had the greatest sequencing depth (median 21,880 UMIs and 3,885 detected genes per cell), making differential dropout an unlikely explanation for a compartment-specific signal."));
add(P("The HPV contrast was confined to the malignant compartment. With the six-gene core score computed per cell, malignant cells differed by 0.103 between HPV groups (AUC = 0.868), whereas every non-malignant cell type, including the T, B and plasma-cell compartments, differed by at most 0.011 in either direction (Fig. 5a, b). In a mixed model of the six-gene core score over all cells, with patient as a random effect and per-cell detection depth as a covariate, the HPV × malignant interaction was 0.090 (P = 8.1 × 10^−112^); within malignant cells alone, the HPV+ effect was 0.124 (95% CI 0.084–0.164). Including MAJIN under its deposited symbol did not alter the result: the per-cell contrast was 0.103 for the six-gene core and 0.117 for the five core genes matched by current symbol alone, with similar discrimination (AUC 0.868 and 0.865). Fig. 5 was generated with the five-gene version; six-gene values are reported in the text and Table 6."));
add(P("The effect was not driven by a single tumour. With malignant cells aggregated per patient, all 7 HPV+ tumours scored above all 13 HPV− tumours on the core score computed from the five genes matched by current symbol (Mann–Whitney P = 1.3 × 10^−5^; Fig. 5e), with the same result for the six-gene core (Table 6), and separation persisted after removal of any single HPV+ patient (largest leave-one-out P = 3.7 × 10^−5^). Pseudobulk expression, a method independent of per-cell scoring, gave the same result: among 6 HPV+ and 13 HPV− tumours with at least 20 malignant cells, core-module pseudobulk expression (five current-symbol genes) was 7.0-fold higher in HPV+ malignant cells (P = 3.7 × 10^−5^), whereas the cancer–testis comparator was unchanged (0.96-fold, P = 0.26) (Fig. 5c). For individual genes, SYCP2 was detected in 35.8% of HPV+ versus 1.5% of HPV− malignant cells and SMC1B in 29.5% versus 0.6% (Fig. 5d); across the panel, 14 of 29 meiotic genes but only 4 of 13 cancer–testis antigens were detected significantly more often in HPV+ malignant cells (FDR < 0.05), reproducing within tumour cells the class asymmetry seen in bulk."));
add(P("The core genes were not uniformly malignant-specific, and the departures were systematic. SYCP2 and SMC1B were detected 7.7- and 11.0-fold more often in malignant cells than in the most-detecting non-malignant compartment, whereas STAG3 showed little enrichment (1.18-fold) and MEI1 was detected more often in B/plasma cells than in malignant cells (0.22-fold; Supplementary Table {ST:specificity}). Both exceptions have documented lymphocyte functions alongside their meiotic roles, which links them directly to the plasma-cell and B-cell enrichment of HPV+ tumours. Excluding these two genes gave the four-gene meiosis-specific score (SMC1B, SYCP2, SYCE2, MAJIN), which removes the lymphoid contribution; all conclusions held for both scores (Table 6)."));
add(FIGURE(SD + "Figure5.png",
  "**Figure 5. The meiotic programme is localised to malignant cells, not the tumour microenvironment (GSE181919; 23,088 primary-tumour cells).** The core module in all panels comprises the five core genes matched by current gene symbol (MAJIN is deposited as C11orf85); six-gene values are given in the text and Table 6. (a) Difference in core module score between HPV+ and HPV− cells, by cell type. (b) Per-cell core module score in malignant cells. (c) Patient-level pseudobulk expression of the core module in malignant cells; point area is proportional to the number of contributing cells, and patients with fewer than 20 malignant cells are omitted. (d) Fraction of malignant cells in which each panel gene is detected, HPV+ against HPV−; the dashed line marks parity. (e) Patient-level effect sizes for the core module (five genes), the wider meiotic panel and the classic CTAs, showing that the contrast is specific to the meiotic machinery rather than a general germ-cell effect.", 840, true));

add(H2("3.12. The malignant-cell localisation replicates in an independent single-cell cohort"));
add(P("Because a single-cell cohort with seven HPV+ tumours cannot support this conclusion alone, the analysis was repeated in GSE182227, an oropharyngeal cohort of 16 tumours (11 HPV+, 5 HPV−) deposited without cell-type or malignant-cell annotations. De novo annotation with the same canonical marker panels resolved nine lineages (Fig. 6a), and the epithelial compartment was divided into malignant and normal epithelium using adjacent-normal tissue fraction and inferred copy number, neither of which involves a meiotic gene. Malignant cells clustered by patient, whereas normal epithelium from different patients clustered together (Fig. 6b). One of 16 epithelial clusters contained 75% of all adjacent-normal epithelial cells in the cohort and had an inferred copy-number score of 0.094, compared with a median of 0.126 across malignant clusters; both criteria classified it as normal. With this assignment, 3.9% of normal epithelial cells (n = 2,127) detected any of the four meiosis-specific genes, compared with 37.2% of 23,709 malignant cells (Fig. 6c); retaining the normal cluster in the malignant set would have substantially inflated apparent meiotic expression in normal epithelium."));
add(P("With the compartment defined, the HPV contrast was reproduced. Per tumour, the four-gene meiosis-specific score was 0.176 higher in HPV+ than in HPV− tumours (AUC = 0.945, 95% CI 0.83–1.00; P = 0.003; Fig. 6d), compared with 0.112 (AUC = 1.000, P = 7.4 × 10^−5^) for the same score in GSE181919, giving the same direction and comparable magnitude in two cohorts annotated by different procedures (Fig. 6e; Table 6). The six-gene core score gave equivalent results in both cohorts (Table 6). The cancer–testis comparator discriminated in neither cohort (AUC = 0.282, 95% CI 0.04–0.52, P = 0.15; AUC = 0.564, 95% CI 0.26–0.87, P = 0.74), nor did the proliferation score alone (AUC = 0.731 and 0.782). Tumour-level separation in GSE182227 was near-complete: two HPV+ tumours fell within the HPV− range (Fig. 6d), including OP13, which contributed only 155 malignant cells and scored marginally below the highest-scoring HPV− tumour (OP8). When the four-gene score was recomputed with an independently written implementation, OP13 versus OP8 was the only discordant pair (AUC = 0.982, P = 9.0 × 10^−4^), and the proportion of malignant cells detecting any of the four meiosis-specific genes separated the groups completely (AUC = 1.000, P = 5.0 × 10^−4^), suggesting that the prevalence of programme-positive cells is a more stable readout than the mean score when few cells are available."));
add(P("Proliferation is an important potential confounder because meiotic and mitotic programmes share components and HPV+ tumours are more proliferative. In the pooled analysis of 35 tumours from both cohorts, adding the proliferation score reduced the HPV coefficient for the four-gene meiosis-specific score from 0.141 (P = 4.5 × 10^−6^) to 0.109 (P = 2.7 × 10^−5^), a 22% attenuation (Fig. 6f; Supplementary Table {ST:prolif}). Proliferation therefore accounted for about one-fifth of the association. The attenuation was larger in GSE182227 (34%) than in GSE181919 (6%), and in the pooled model proliferation had an independent coefficient of 0.359 (P = 2.8 × 10^−4^), supporting separability of the two associations. The pooled adjusted estimate was robust to the regression method: heteroskedasticity-consistent standard errors gave P = 1.3 × 10^−4^, and Huber robust regression gave a coefficient of 0.097 (P = 9.1 × 10^−8^)."));
add(TCAP("**Table 6.** HPV contrast in per-tumour module scores in both single-cell cohorts, malignant cells only. Δ, difference in mean score between HPV+ and HPV− tumours; P, two-sided Mann–Whitney test on tumour-level means. These tumour-level contrasts are not directly comparable with the per-cell contrasts in Fig. 5a."));
