add(FIGURE(SD + "Figure6.png",
  "**Figure 6. Independent replication in GSE182227 (16 oropharyngeal tumours, annotated de novo).** (a) Lineage annotation from canonical markers. (b) Malignant cells, coloured by HPV status, cluster by patient, whereas normal epithelium is shared across patients, the property used to separate the two. (c) Percentage of cells detecting any of the four meiosis-specific genes, by compartment, after the epithelial split. (d) Per-tumour four-gene meiosis-specific score by HPV status; two HPV+ tumours fall within the HPV− range (see text). (e) The same four-gene contrast in both single-cell cohorts. (f) HPV effect on the four-gene meiosis-specific score with and without adjustment for proliferation, by cohort and pooled; bars are 95% confidence intervals.", 850, true));

add(H2("3.13. Exploratory univariable survival analysis"));
add(P("All 279 tumours in the transcriptomic cohort had overall-survival annotation (140 deaths; Table 1). In exploratory univariable Cox models, higher expression of seven of the nine leading HPV-upregulated genes was nominally associated with longer overall survival, with HRs per standard deviation between 0.70 (ZFR2; 95% CI 0.56–0.87) and 0.82 (SMC1B and GRIN2C); associations for TDRD10 and RAD9B did not reach nominal significance (Supplementary Table {ST:cox}; Supplementary Fig. {SF:forest}). Kaplan–Meier curves for ZFR2, split at median expression, are shown in Supplementary Fig. {SF:km}. These models were not adjusted for HPV status, stage, smoking or treatment and were not corrected for multiple testing. Because the genes are strongly HPV-associated and HPV+ patients had markedly better outcomes in this cohort (Table 1), the associations are likely to reflect HPV status to a large extent and should not be interpreted as independent prognostic effects."));

// ---------------- DISCUSSION ----------------
add(H1("4. Discussion"));
add(P("HPV+ HNSCC differed from HPV− disease along three transcriptional axes: induction of the meiotic machinery, an adaptive-immune and plasma-cell-rich microenvironment, and reduced keratinisation and epithelial-differentiation programmes. The central finding concerns the first. Germ-cell genes expressed in HPV+ HNSCC were drawn preferentially from the meiotic machinery rather than representing a random subset of germline genes; they were co-expressed as a module, and their promoters showed demethylation that tracked their expression. These observations are inconsistent with the stochastic de-repression model proposed for cancer–testis antigen expression [https://doi.org/10.1002/gcc.20160; https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkn673]. The comparison was made within the same tumours using the pre-specified MAGE/CTAG comparator, which showed no discriminatory signal (AUC = 0.411), whereas the synaptonemal complex reached an AUC of 0.930."));
add(P("The association with viral transcriptional activity is the most mechanistically informative result. HPV DNA-positive tumours without viral transcription resembled HPV− tumours on the core meiotic score (P = 0.56), whereas virus-active tumours were clearly separated. This ordering places the programme downstream of E6/E7 expression rather than of viral DNA presence and is consistent with the reported capacity of E7 to induce locus-specific promoter methylation changes [https://doi.org/10.1111/cas.12761]. It also has a practical implication: studies relying on HPV DNA status alone may underestimate associations of this type, as shown by the fall in AUC from 0.959 to 0.753 when DNA-based classification was applied to the same samples."));
add(P("Two non-exclusive explanations could account for the preferential and coordinated activation of the meiotic machinery. One is shared regulatory architecture, whereby the core meiotic genes are governed by a common germline-restricted programme whose methylation-dependent repression is relieved together; this would account for both the specificity and the coherence of the programme. A concrete candidate exists. In somatic cells, meiosis-specific genes carry a conserved E2F6-binding element in their promoters [https://doi.org/10.1095/biolreprod.108.067645], E2F6 silences SMC1B and STAG3 [https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M506797200], and E2F6 recruits DNMT3B to establish methylation at germline gene promoters [https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1000473107]; no comparable E2F6 dependence has been described for the MAGE/CTAG family, whose silencing is attributed mainly to promoter DNA methylation [https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0058743]. Because E7 disrupts RB–E2F control [https://doi.org/10.1038/nrc2886], interference with E2F6-dependent repression would provide a single mechanism for the class specificity, the coordination and the dependence on viral transcription observed here. This predicts reduced E2F6 or DNMT3B occupancy at core meiotic promoters in E7-expressing cells, and de-repression of the core genes, but not of MAGE/CTAG genes, after E2F6 depletion. The near-equal coherence in transcriptionally silent HPV+ tumours (0.541) and active ones (0.556) would also fit co-regulation that precedes activation, although the silent group was not tested against its own size-matched null. The other is functional selection. Ectopic meiotic cohesin generates chromosomal instability [https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2204071119], ectopically expressed HORMAD1 has been linked to genomic instability and homologous-recombination deficiency [https://doi.org/10.1158/2159-8290.CD-14-1092; https://doi.org/10.3390/cells12121627] and to protection of stalled replication forks [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbc.2023.105348], and partial activation of the meiotic programme has been proposed to be oncogenic [https://doi.org/10.1080/15384101.2020.1743902]. Under this model, the meiotic module could be retained because it confers an advantage to tumour cells rather than simply reflecting passive demethylation. Distinguishing these explanations will require perturbation experiments."));
add(P("The immune findings place this programme in context. HPV+ tumours showed higher estimated plasma-cell and CD8+ T-cell fractions and lower M0 macrophage, resting NK-cell and resting CD4+ memory T-cell fractions, supported by correlations of the estimates with lineage marker genes and by upregulation of immunoglobulin and B-cell transcripts that persisted after adjustment for primary site. B-cell- and plasma-cell-rich infiltrates and tertiary lymphoid structures have been associated with favourable outcome and immunotherapy response in other tumour types [https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-019-1914-8; https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-019-1906-8], and a lymphocyte-rich microenvironment is a recognised feature of HPV+ HNSCC [https://doi.org/10.1038/nrc.2018.11]. The lymphoid-rich oropharynx, where most HPV+ tumours arise, probably contributes to the plasma-cell signal, and these are relative, computationally inferred fractions rather than direct cell counts. In this study the immune differences also define the compositional confound that a bulk meiotic signal must overcome, and the single-cell data address it. The HPV contrast was confined to malignant cells and was essentially absent from the lymphocyte populations that differ most in abundance between HPV+ and HPV− tumours. Within HPV+ tumours, core meiotic genes were also detected more often in malignant than in non-malignant cells of the same specimen, arguing against a tumour-wide field effect. The two exceptions to malignant-cell specificity, MEI1 and STAG3, were detected in B/plasma cells, exactly where the bulk immune analysis would predict contamination, and excluding them did not change the conclusion. That the same localisation was observed in a second cohort annotated de novo, with its malignant compartment defined by copy number and tissue of origin rather than deposited labels, makes an annotation artefact unlikely."));
add(P("The reduced expression of keratinisation, cornified-envelope and epithelial-differentiation genes in HPV+ tumours is consistent with the non-keratinising histology and tonsillar-crypt origin of most HPV+ oropharyngeal carcinomas [https://doi.org/10.1038/nrc.2018.11]. It persisted after adjustment for site and serves as an internal check that the transcriptomic contrast captures established HPV-associated biology. Whether loss of squamous differentiation is related to meiotic reactivation cannot be determined from these data."));
add(P("These findings bear on how this gene class is used translationally. Cancer–testis antigens are pursued as immunotherapeutic targets largely because of their tumour-restricted expression [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tranon.2019.02.008; https://doi.org/10.1158/0008-5472.CAN-15-2125]. In HPV+ HNSCC, the MAGE/CTAG family did not distinguish tumours by HPV status, whereas the meiotic structural and recombination components did, were more tightly co-expressed, and were expressed in malignant cells within a lymphocyte-rich microenvironment. This is an argument about target selection based on expression specificity, not evidence of immunogenicity, which was not assessed. The exploratory survival analysis adds little to this argument: several leading HPV-associated genes were nominally associated with longer overall survival, but the models were univariable and not adjusted for HPV status, stage, smoking or treatment, so the associations probably reflect the better prognosis of HPV+ disease. Whether meiotic-programme activity carries prognostic information within HPV+ tumours requires larger, adequately adjusted cohorts."));
add(P("The single-cell data do not indicate uniform activation across malignant cells. Even in HPV+ tumours, individual meiotic transcripts were detected in a minority of malignant cells; SYCP2 had the highest detection rate (35.8%). Limited detection partly reflects the sensitivity of 3′ droplet-based sequencing, but activation restricted to a malignant subpopulation cannot be excluded. Module scores and patient-level aggregates are therefore more robust measures of programme activation than single-cell detection, and higher-sensitivity approaches such as full-length or targeted single-cell sequencing will be needed to resolve this question."));
add(H2("4.1. Provenance and circularity"));
add(P("The panel was constructed after the genome-wide enrichment analysis had identified meiosis as a dominant theme, so the overlap statistics in Supplementary Fig. {SF:origin}c quantify the magnitude of an effect in the same samples that motivated panel construction and are not independent evidence. Three considerations limit the consequences of this dependence. First, panel membership was defined from the meiosis literature and CTdatabase conventions rather than from the differential-expression results, and 49 of the 86 testable panel genes were not upregulated. Second, the substantive conclusions concern the specificity and structure of germ-cell expression rather than its presence: concentration in the meiotic machinery rather than the MAGE/CTAG family, coordinated behaviour, and dependence on viral transcriptional activity. None of these follows from the enrichment analysis, and the class contrast is present genome-wide (odds ratio 10.9), not only within the curated panel. Third, the discriminative, coordination and methylation findings were established in GSE65858 and GSE38266, and the malignant-cell localisation in GSE181919 and GSE182227, none of which contributed to panel construction."));

// ---------------- LIMITATIONS ----------------
add(H1("5. Limitations"));
add(P("This study has several limitations. The TCGA transcriptomic cohort contains only 36 HPV+ tumours, and the single-cell evidence rests on 35 tumours in two cohorts (17 HPV+, 18 HPV−), both generated with 3′ droplet-based sequencing, which limits detection of lowly expressed transcripts. Malignant-cell annotations in GSE181919 were taken from the original study, whereas those in GSE182227 were assigned without external ground truth, although the two criteria used were concordant; tumour-level discrimination in GSE182227 was not complete, with two HPV+ tumours within the HPV− range (AUC = 0.945)."));
add(P("The methylation replication cohort was small (42 samples), with only one locus surviving per-gene Bonferroni correction, so replication rests on effect-size concordance (ρ = 0.688) and the set-level test (P = 6.8 × 10^−4^) rather than on individual loci. Evidence implicating demethylation remains correlative; directionality, per-sample association and replication are consistent with it, but causal inference requires demethylating-agent or targeted epigenetic-editing experiments. Gene-level methylation summarised as mean β across all annotated probes is a coarse measure that may obscure CpG-island substructure and allele-specific effects, and the promoter-proximal probe set was applied only to the GSE38266 set-level test; a single promoter definition (for example TSS1500, TSS200, 5′UTR and first exon) was not applied across cohorts. MEI1 and STAG3, which are also expressed in B and plasma cells, are included in all bulk analyses (the GSE65858 discrimination and coherence results and the TCGA methylation composite), and MEI1 showed the largest methylation difference; because HPV+ tumours are richer in B and plasma cells, part of these bulk signals may reflect cell composition, and the bulk methylation analyses were not adjusted for B-cell or plasma-cell fractions. The malignant-cell localisation was confirmed with the four-gene score that excludes these genes, but only in the single-cell cohorts. Expression was measured on different platforms in the discovery (RNA-seq) and validation (microarray) cohorts, so effect sizes are not directly comparable. Two HPV definitions were used within TCGA, RNA-seq-based for the transcriptomic cohort and p16/ISH-based for the panel analyses, which is why the sample counts of these analyses differ; because p16 positivity outside the oropharynx is often unrelated to HPV, the p16-or-ISH rule may misclassify some non-oropharyngeal tumours, and a sensitivity analysis requiring ISH positivity was not performed. AUC confidence intervals were obtained with the Hanley–McNeil approximation rather than DeLong or bootstrap methods."));
add(P("Immune-cell fractions were inferred computationally, are relative rather than absolute, are limited to the 22 LM22 populations and were not confirmed at the protein level; the CD8/M2 ratio is an exploratory derived score. Although differential expression was adjusted for primary site in a sensitivity analysis, residual site confounding of the immune estimates cannot be excluded given the oropharyngeal predominance of HPV+ tumours. The survival analyses were univariable, limited to nine candidate genes selected for their HPV association, not adjusted for HPV status, stage, smoking or treatment, and not corrected for multiple testing; they are hypothesis-generating only. The gene panel was defined after the discovery analysis had identified meiosis as the dominant theme, so its overlap with the discovery output (37 of 86 testable genes upregulated) indicates magnitude rather than independent evidence, although the class contrast and all external-cohort findings are unaffected. Finally, all cohorts are retrospective and observational and were drawn predominantly from North American and European populations, so the associations reported cannot be interpreted causally and may not generalise to other populations."));

// ---------------- CONCLUSIONS ----------------
add(H1("6. Conclusions"));
add(P("Transcriptionally active HPV+ head and neck squamous cell carcinoma is associated with a specific, internally coordinated meiotic gene programme, accompanied by promoter demethylation and dependent on viral transcriptional activity rather than viral presence. The classic cancer–testis antigens, tested as a pre-specified comparator in the same tumours, show none of these properties. HPV+ tumours also have a plasma-cell- and CD8+ T-cell-rich immune microenvironment and reduced keratinisation programmes, yet single-cell analysis in two independent cohorts localises the meiotic programme to malignant cells, and the association persists after adjustment for proliferation. These data establish associations rather than causal direction. They indicate that germ-cell gene expression in this disease is better described as a directed epigenetic programme than as stochastic de-repression, and that the meiotic machinery, rather than the MAGE/CTAG family, is the more informative compartment for mechanistic and translational study."));

